\title{Proving a function is a bijection}
\date{2026-03-10T11:25:05Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Remark}
\p{Normally, to prove a function is a [bijection](00AG), we can show it is both [1-1](00A0) and [onto](00A8). Alternatively, it turns out we can show it has an [inverse](00AG). However, we must be careful: to prove #{g} is the inverse of #{f}, we have to prove \em{both} #{g(f(a)) = a} for all #{a} in the domain of #{f}, \em{and} #{f(g(b)) = b} for all #{b} in the codomain. (\em{Exercise}: prove that if there is a function #{g} such that #{g(f(a)) = a} for all #{a} in the domain of #{f}, then #{f} is injective; likewise, if there is #{g} such that #{f(g(b)) = b} for all #{b} in the codomain, then #{f} is surjective.)}
